1
00:00:18,695 --> 00:00:19,675
Good evening.

2
00:00:19,696 --> 00:00:21,599
I trust you had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:25,064
I do not say happy, though I'm sure it has been a happy day.

4
00:00:25,123 --> 00:00:48,616
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambho Dasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambho Dasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha

5
00:00:48,917 --> 00:00:57,406
Sam Buddha Sat, homage to the Buddha, the blessed noble and fully self-awakened one.

6
00:00:57,466 --> 00:01:15,887
So yesterday, the discourse we were tackling was the fear that people had that the order would go into some huge arguments and disruption once the Buddha died, in the same way that the James had done.

7
00:01:16,188 --> 00:01:16,929
if they had done.

8
00:01:16,948 --> 00:01:19,111
Remember, this is Buddhist propaganda.

9
00:01:19,894 --> 00:01:23,338
The lights keep going off and on for some spooky reason.

10
00:01:23,539 --> 00:01:26,924
So sometimes I'll be in the dark and sometimes I'll be in the light.

11
00:01:28,286 --> 00:01:29,067
I'll be enlightened.

12
00:01:30,228 --> 00:01:35,858
So now we're at Kosambi in Gosita's park.

13
00:01:37,379 --> 00:01:45,132
Now at that time, the bhikkhus, the Monaster bhikkhus, the monks, were quarreling and brawling and were in deep disputes

14
00:01:45,533 --> 00:01:47,674
stabbing each other with verbal daggers.

15
00:01:48,756 --> 00:01:57,144
They could neither convince each other nor be convinced by others, nor could neither persuade each other nor be persuaded by the other.

16
00:01:58,887 --> 00:02:07,656
And it got so bad that they went and, of course, somebody snitched on them to the body, to the Blessed One.

17
00:02:08,176 --> 00:02:09,377
So, of course, he called them.

18
00:02:09,457 --> 00:02:10,900
He went to see them, actually.

19
00:02:11,402 --> 00:02:15,347
And he said, is it true that you've been quarreling and brawling and all that sort of stuff?

20
00:02:15,366 --> 00:02:16,828
And he said, yes, Venerable Sir.

21
00:02:16,848 --> 00:02:19,211
He says, what do you think, you see?

22
00:02:19,751 --> 00:02:21,393
And then he goes into this discourse.

23
00:02:21,432 --> 00:02:26,918
Now, this brawling was all about the Vinaya.

24
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:37,207
So you might think to yourself, well, this big argument, which has really created this huge anger within the monastery,

25
00:02:37,794 --> 00:03:07,413
uh you know people shouting at each other and going into all sorts of disgraceful um actions uh you might think this is very important but actually it was because the teacher of the dharma so the teacher of the dharma and a teacher of the veneer of the discipline of the rules and regulations the uh the dharma teacher had not emptied the water pot in the toilet uh he should have emptied it it seems

26
00:03:07,933 --> 00:03:17,269
Now, when the Vinaya, Dara, the person who understands Vinaya, saw this, he said, now that's an offence.

27
00:03:17,549 --> 00:03:20,533
See, that's an offence, unless you didn't mean it.

28
00:03:21,094 --> 00:03:23,237
So the Dharma teacher said, well, I didn't mean it.

29
00:03:23,418 --> 00:03:25,442
And he probably didn't even know.

30
00:03:25,502 --> 00:03:26,984
But anyway, it was an offence.

31
00:03:27,604 --> 00:03:32,693
Anyway, later on, the Vinaya teacher was talking to his students, telling them about this occasion.

32
00:03:33,348 --> 00:03:39,514
And they eventually came to the conclusion that even though it was unintentional, it was still a offense.

33
00:03:40,195 --> 00:03:44,981
So then the Dharma teachers, pupils and all that got really upset about that.

34
00:03:45,002 --> 00:03:52,691
And before you know it, quarreling and all sorts of bad things, stabbing each other with verbal doubts.

35
00:03:55,294 --> 00:03:57,836
Now you might think to yourself, well, that's ridiculous.

36
00:03:57,897 --> 00:03:59,800
How could anybody get angry and all that?

37
00:04:00,139 --> 00:04:02,443
But to consider the arguments that

38
00:04:02,861 --> 00:04:11,973
Uh, perhaps you've not been in, but, uh, I remember other people and occasionally I myself have fallen into arguments as to whether you leave the toilet seat up or down.

39
00:04:12,493 --> 00:04:12,713
Yeah.

40
00:04:13,816 --> 00:04:27,872
Uh, whether, uh, whether you put the milk in before you pour the tea or after you pour the tea, whether a cup of tea is finished when you finish the tea and you just leave the pots hanging around or when you wash the pots and put them away.

41
00:04:28,613 --> 00:04:31,737
So, you know, and, and there was one I remember from childhood.

42
00:04:33,827 --> 00:04:36,992
My father was in the Royal Air Force during the war.

43
00:04:37,432 --> 00:04:40,476
And of course, and probably his family did it this way.

44
00:04:40,596 --> 00:04:44,040
But in the military, of course, you have to do things in a certain way.

45
00:04:44,661 --> 00:04:51,730
Now, when it came to polishing shoes and boots, you put the polish on with the cloth and you shine it up with the brush.

46
00:04:52,132 --> 00:04:53,874
Well, my mother was not going to have anything to that.

47
00:04:53,894 --> 00:04:56,476
She wasn't going to mess around with cloth brushes.

48
00:04:56,497 --> 00:04:59,480
You just put it on with a brush and brushed it up.

49
00:04:59,663 --> 00:05:05,690
And I remember as a child this roaring argument in the kitchen about how to put the polish on shoes.

50
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:07,012
And they were really at each other.

51
00:05:07,132 --> 00:05:07,934
Can you imagine that?

52
00:05:08,855 --> 00:05:13,341
So these, whether we like it or not, we've got these little covert rules.

53
00:05:15,103 --> 00:05:28,523
When a friend or somebody comes and visits us and starts doing things like putting dirty pots directly into the water that's in the bowl rather than put them on the side,

54
00:05:29,127 --> 00:05:31,170
Oh, argumentation.

55
00:05:31,670 --> 00:05:36,437
So little things can spark into these huge arguments.

56
00:05:36,497 --> 00:05:40,223
And of course, we don't know the history of these two monks.

57
00:05:40,242 --> 00:05:41,644
They might not like each other.

58
00:05:42,225 --> 00:05:44,488
They might have been in some sort of argument before.

59
00:05:44,850 --> 00:05:47,293
By the time the Buddha gets there, it's a big deal.

60
00:05:48,154 --> 00:05:54,624
Now, he says to them, you know, he tries to give them advice.

61
00:05:54,944 --> 00:05:55,846
They won't listen to him.

62
00:05:56,005 --> 00:05:56,786
So he goes off.

63
00:05:56,826 --> 00:05:58,829
He goes off and lives in the forest.

64
00:05:59,333 --> 00:06:03,057
And while he lives in the forest, he's looked after by an elephant who brings him fruits.

65
00:06:03,458 --> 00:06:08,564
And when a monkey saw the elephant doing that, the monkey also went off and got him food.

66
00:06:09,826 --> 00:06:12,689
Now, of course, that sort of story is far-fetched for us.

67
00:06:14,432 --> 00:06:22,161
But if you look, if you see some of the videos that are on the YouTube, some things are quite remarkable.

68
00:06:22,622 --> 00:06:24,766
And there's one that comes to mind.

69
00:06:24,906 --> 00:06:26,487
Somebody told me that...

70
00:06:26,788 --> 00:06:38,627
They had visited this farm or something like that, a friend or whatever, and the horse was just in the barn.

71
00:06:38,666 --> 00:06:41,170
The door was open, but the horse was just standing in the barn.

72
00:06:41,630 --> 00:06:53,809
They parked the car and they opened the boot and in the boot they put the cradle for the child, the little baby, and off they went to have a chat in the house.

73
00:06:54,295 --> 00:07:02,144
When they came back, because they could hear the baby crying a bit, when they came back, the horse had come out and was rocking the cradle with its nose.

74
00:07:03,005 --> 00:07:05,389
Fancy that.

75
00:07:08,372 --> 00:07:20,708
You can also find, I've got it on one of the web pages, extraordinary things, I can't remember the name of the web page, but there's a woman who can directly communicate with animals.

76
00:07:22,038 --> 00:07:46,932
anyway that's to the side that's to the side the point is that when the lay people found out that the buddha had not been listened to that the monks weren't ready to come to an agreement they stopped feeding the monks they stopped serving the monks you see now that's the power of the lay people because the monastic orders monks and nuns are entirely dependent upon

77
00:07:47,197 --> 00:07:52,949
the gifts that lay people give them, the food, the clothing, the medicine, the housing, etc.

78
00:07:53,310 --> 00:08:04,853
So if lay people decide that these monastics are not behaving well and they don't support them, then basically they've either got to go and find another monastery or leave the order.

79
00:08:06,697 --> 00:08:08,581
So, of course, that brought them to the senses.

80
00:08:09,827 --> 00:08:11,149
And they bring the Buddha.

81
00:08:11,589 --> 00:08:13,812
They ask the Buddha to come back, the blessed one.

82
00:08:14,334 --> 00:08:15,394
And then he talks to them.

83
00:08:16,156 --> 00:08:17,937
And this is what he says.

84
00:08:21,663 --> 00:08:33,658
Did you on that occasion, when you were arguing and all that, did you maintain acts of loving kindness of body, speech and mind in public and private towards your companions in the holy life?

85
00:08:34,239 --> 00:08:35,159
No, venerable sir.

86
00:08:38,346 --> 00:08:47,456
And then he says there are six memorable qualities that create love and respect and conduce to helpfulness, to non-dispute, to concord and to unity.

87
00:08:48,176 --> 00:08:59,587
The first three are pretty straightforward to maintain bodily acts of loving kindness, both in public and in private, towards your companions, your friends, etc.

88
00:09:00,749 --> 00:09:03,671
The second one is the verbal acts of loving kindness.

89
00:09:04,312 --> 00:09:07,375
The third, the mental acts of loving kindness.

90
00:09:08,788 --> 00:09:24,408
Now the fourth is sharing with them any gain of a kind that accords with the Dharma and has been obtained in a way that accords with the Dharma, including even the contents of your bowls.

91
00:09:25,029 --> 00:09:37,125
So in other words, sharing what we have is a really lovely way to create that relationship where it's difficult to get into big arguments.

92
00:09:40,345 --> 00:09:43,448
And then, of course, to live a virtuous life.

93
00:09:44,070 --> 00:09:59,067
So the virtues that are unbroken, untorn, unblotched, unmottled, liberating, commended by the wise, not misapprehended and conducive to concentration or to meditation.

94
00:09:59,828 --> 00:10:03,432
So that people are affected, are

95
00:10:03,768 --> 00:10:28,914
uh should we say they they honor and respect people who are virtuous you know and who come across virtues i just think of politicians for instance and then and then there's the uh the holy life that is uh that view so this is that understanding that a person holds the understanding that the the view

96
00:10:29,164 --> 00:10:38,057
that is noble and emancipating, leads one to practices in accordance with it to the complete destruction of suffering.

97
00:10:38,379 --> 00:10:46,851
So that the view is, of course, the one of the Eightfold Noble Path, the Noble Eightfold Path.

98
00:10:47,712 --> 00:10:52,921
All these things are conducive to helpfulness, non-dispute, concord, and to unity.

99
00:10:55,664 --> 00:10:57,567
But then he goes on to say,

100
00:10:57,970 --> 00:11:12,067
But the highest of all these six is that view that is noble and emancipating and leads the one who practices in accordance with it to the complete destruction of suffering.

101
00:11:12,568 --> 00:11:17,936
So, I mean, in a sense, the Eiffel Noble path includes everything he just said.

102
00:11:21,801 --> 00:11:24,965
And how does one practice with that?

103
00:11:25,205 --> 00:11:26,046
Okay.

104
00:11:26,066 --> 00:11:27,488
How does one get to this point?

105
00:11:28,970 --> 00:11:32,394
Or how do you put this into practice, this view?

106
00:11:33,777 --> 00:11:38,504
So, gone to the forest, to the root of a tree, to an empty hut, in other words, to meditate.

107
00:11:38,524 --> 00:11:48,238
So, in other words, to bring this into everyday life as well as the sitting, but in everything that we do to bring the Eightfold Noble Path.

108
00:11:50,140 --> 00:11:51,202
Noble Eightfold Path.

109
00:11:51,322 --> 00:11:52,024
I keep saying that, don't I?

110
00:11:54,129 --> 00:12:11,216
So a person might be obsessed with sensual lust, sensual desire, obsessed by ill will, obsessed by sloth and torpor, obsessed by restlessness and remorse, obsessed by doubt.

111
00:12:11,235 --> 00:12:15,201
So these, of course, are your five hindrances, which, of course, you know.

112
00:12:15,241 --> 00:12:22,432
And the next one is that not to be absorbed in speculation about the world.

113
00:12:23,003 --> 00:12:38,345
Oh my goodness, COVID, Brexit, the world, too much, not to be absorbed in that sort of speculation and not to be absorbed in the speculation of the other world.

114
00:12:38,645 --> 00:12:42,451
So I don't think that bothers we Westerners very much.

115
00:12:42,510 --> 00:12:46,876
We don't tend to think about the next life, except something might happen.

116
00:12:47,097 --> 00:12:48,639
And of course, something might not.

117
00:12:53,799 --> 00:13:17,006
So those are the things that he's obsessed with, the five hindrances, which are, of course, sensual desire, ill will, sloth and torpor, restlessness and remorse, doubt, that skeptical doubt, the doubt that stops you doing something, speculation about the world, worries about the world, all that sort of stuff, and about future lives.

118
00:13:19,163 --> 00:13:36,600
Now, when he comes to understand that no obsession is unabandoned in myself, that no obsession unabandoned in myself that might so obsess my mind that I cannot know and see things as they actually are.

119
00:13:36,620 --> 00:13:43,687
So now the heart's been purified to a point that it can really begin to see things as they really are.

120
00:13:44,408 --> 00:13:48,613
My mind is well disposed for awakening to the Four Noble Truths.

121
00:13:50,990 --> 00:13:56,315
When I pursue, develop and cultivate this view, I personally obtain serenity.

122
00:13:56,936 --> 00:13:58,256
I obtain quenching.

123
00:13:58,837 --> 00:14:02,120
That means the lessening of these desires.

124
00:14:04,323 --> 00:14:14,293
He also understands that there is no recluse or Brahmin outside the Buddha's dispensation, which is possessed of a view such as I possess.

125
00:14:14,313 --> 00:14:17,216
So that's the confidence in the Buddha's teachings.

126
00:14:18,967 --> 00:14:25,056
And then there's that ability, do I possess the character of a person who possesses right view?

127
00:14:25,917 --> 00:14:36,890
There's that ability to know when you've done something wrong, to accept it, confesses, reveals and discloses it here to the teacher or to wise companions.

128
00:14:37,412 --> 00:14:42,477
But basically the ability to know you've done something wrong and to put it right, you know.

129
00:14:42,727 --> 00:14:44,289
to apologize, et cetera.

130
00:14:44,931 --> 00:14:52,419
Just as a young tender infant lying prone at once draws back when he puts his foot or hand on a live coal.

131
00:14:52,740 --> 00:15:04,014
So in other words, any time we approach anything which is unwholesome, unhelpful, unbeneficial to ourselves, to others, we just sort of bounce off it.

132
00:15:05,216 --> 00:15:08,500
So that's the sensitivity of a virtuous person.

133
00:15:12,142 --> 00:15:27,384
Although he may be active in various matters for his companions in the holy life, yet he has a keen regard for training in the higher virtue, training in the higher mind, training in the higher wisdom.

134
00:15:27,865 --> 00:15:32,692
Just as a cow with a new calf, while she gazes, watches her calf.

135
00:15:32,974 --> 00:15:33,794
So basically,

136
00:15:34,011 --> 00:15:44,909
Even though we're busy in our daily lives, there's always that inclination to keep an eye on our awareness, on our mindfulness and, of course, our practice.

137
00:15:46,113 --> 00:15:49,918
So these are the five things that are conducive.

138
00:15:51,642 --> 00:15:58,212
Which is to know that these obsessions have been abandoned or, you know, we're close to it.

139
00:15:59,289 --> 00:16:06,157
To pursue, develop and cultivate, that I personally obtain serenity, personally obtain quenching, that's number two.

140
00:16:06,576 --> 00:16:13,423
So in other words, feeling calmer about my life, being able to understand myself and how the world works, etc.

141
00:16:14,304 --> 00:16:15,666
Spiritually speaking, I mean.

142
00:16:17,327 --> 00:16:27,778
That I have this confidence in the Buddha's teachings, that as soon as I do something wrong, I'll put it right, I apologize and put it right.

143
00:16:28,855 --> 00:16:42,570
that I'm constantly moving, I'm constantly inclining, as the Buddha would say, inclining to Nibbana, which means looking at my life, constantly being aware, mindful.

144
00:16:42,970 --> 00:16:46,033
And it's not that you're being self-critical.

145
00:16:46,072 --> 00:16:52,159
You're not supposed to be hunting yourself down and pointing at yourself and criticizing yourself.

146
00:16:52,179 --> 00:16:56,283
It's just the relaxation into present moment mindfulness.

147
00:16:56,303 --> 00:16:56,725
That's all.

148
00:16:56,945 --> 00:16:58,086
And every time it wanders,

149
00:16:58,403 --> 00:17:00,884
Just keep bringing it back very patiently.

150
00:17:04,308 --> 00:17:07,070
And then do I possess the strength of one?

151
00:17:09,273 --> 00:17:22,365
So he said, yes, the strength comes when on hearing the Dharma, he heeds it, gives it attention, engages with it with all his mind, hears the Dharma with eager eyes.

152
00:17:23,728 --> 00:17:26,250
So that's when a person knows he's got the strength there.

153
00:17:26,534 --> 00:17:28,336
from the right view.

154
00:17:28,375 --> 00:17:44,834
And the second one is that when he listens to the Dharma and when he practices virtue, he gains inspiration in the meaning, gains inspiration in the Dharma, gains gladness collected to the Dharma.

155
00:17:44,854 --> 00:17:48,858
In other words, it gives your life a certain deep meaningfulness.

156
00:17:51,942 --> 00:17:55,326
When a noble disciple is thus possessed of seven factors,

157
00:17:55,728 --> 00:18:00,154
he has well sought the character for realization of the fruit of stream entry.

158
00:18:01,738 --> 00:18:20,148
So in other words, these are the sorts of reflections that a person can have, which leads them to the first attainment of stream entry, which is that moment when somebody actually perceives in some way or other the truth of Nibbāna.

159
00:18:21,901 --> 00:18:31,337
So, of course, here this is what the Blessed One said and the bhikkhus were satisfied and in this particular case were delighted in the Blessed One's words.

160
00:18:32,599 --> 00:18:34,983
So this is the Buddha.

161
00:18:35,023 --> 00:18:40,534
He's been brought into a fierce argument between these monastics.

162
00:18:41,037 --> 00:18:42,097
He's tried to do his best.

163
00:18:42,138 --> 00:18:42,980
They won't listen to him.

164
00:18:43,059 --> 00:18:43,800
Can you imagine that?

165
00:18:43,901 --> 00:18:44,701
They won't listen to him.

166
00:18:45,022 --> 00:18:46,223
So he goes away.

167
00:18:46,744 --> 00:18:49,127
The lay people are shocked by this.

168
00:18:49,308 --> 00:18:50,691
They stop supporting the monks.

169
00:18:50,711 --> 00:18:52,153
The monks come to their senses.

170
00:18:52,712 --> 00:18:55,136
They bring the Buddha back and he gives them this teaching.

171
00:18:56,238 --> 00:18:59,221
And hopefully that puts an end to all these silly arguments.

172
00:19:00,463 --> 00:19:06,593
So, you know, a lot of this stuff pertains to ordinary daily life, ordinary daily life in society.

173
00:19:07,134 --> 00:19:10,317
So I can only hope that it has been inspirational.

174
00:19:10,602 --> 00:19:25,282
and that you're very persistent in your bringing yourself back into this present moment and that by doing so you will be liberated from all suffering sooner rather than later.

175
00:19:27,526 --> 00:19:28,627
So we can do a bit of practice.

